A quick note: condition variables and their associated lock types appear to be outside the Week 6 syllabus scope, so you may want to verify with your course materials that this topic is covered in your current unit. That said, it is a standard C++ concept, so here is a conceptual scaffold.

**[ANALOGY_SCAFFOLD]**

Think of a condition variable like a waiting room at a doctor's office. A patient (thread) enters the waiting room and sits down only after they have checked in at the front desk (acquired a lock). When the nurse calls them (notifies the condition variable), they get up and re-check with the front desk before proceeding. The key point is: you cannot even enter the waiting room without first interacting with the front desk.

In C++, the "front desk" is a `std::unique_lock<std::mutex>`. A `std::condition_variable` requires this specific lock type — not a `std::lock_guard` — because the condition variable needs to be able to **atomically release** the lock while the thread waits, and **reacquire** it upon waking up. A `std::lock_guard` does not support that mid-scope release.

Here is the core pairing in the standard library:

```cpp
std::mutex mtx;
std::condition_variable cv;
std::unique_lock<std::mutex> lk(mtx);
cv.wait(lk, [] { return ready; });
```

Now, a guiding question to check your understanding: why do you think `std::lock_guard` cannot be used here, given what you know about its design compared to `std::unique_lock`?

